# Week 2 Report: Baseline Model for Scene Classification

## Work Completed This Week

During Week 2, the dataset was fully prepared for modeling and a baseline deep learning model was trained and evaluated.

**Main tasks completed:**

- Loaded and cleaned Intel Image Classification dataset (25,000 images)
- Converted images to 150×150 pixel size and normalized pixel values (0-1 range)
- Encoded scene labels (buildings, forest, glacier, mountain, sea, street)
- Split dataset into training, validation, and test sets (70/15/15)
- Trained **CNN baseline model** (Convolutional Neural Network)
- Evaluated model using validation set
- Generated classification report and confusion matrix

---

## Dataset Split

The dataset was split as follows:

| Split | Number of samples |
|-------|-------------------|
| Training set | 17,500 images |
| Validation set | 3,750 images |
| Test set | 3,750 images |

**Total:** 25,000 images

Stratified splitting was used to preserve class balance across all subsets.

---

## Baseline Model

A **Convolutional Neural Network (CNN)** was used as the baseline approach.

**Model architecture:**

| Layer | Details |
|-------|---------|
| Conv2D | 32 filters, 3×3, ReLU |
| MaxPooling2D | 2×2 |
| Conv2D | 64 filters, 3×3, ReLU |
| MaxPooling2D | 2×2 |
| Conv2D | 128 filters, 3×3, ReLU |
| MaxPooling2D | 2×2 |
| Flatten | Convert to vector |
| Dense | 256 neurons, ReLU |
| Dropout | 0.5 (prevent overfitting) |
| Dense | 6 neurons, Softmax (output) |

**Input:** 150×150×3 RGB images
**Output:** 6 scene classes

This model serves as a deep learning benchmark before applying advanced methods (Transfer Learning).

